================================================================================
TEST T43853 -- IPv4_DHCPServer - DHCP 120-day lease
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-15 15:32-15:40 NZDT (steps 0-4 15:32-15:35, the reboot persistence check
          15:36-15:39). The DUT runs UTC and the box NZDT (UTC+13), so DUT timestamps are
          box time minus 13 h. The DUT RTC had been found reading March 2000 and was set
          with the exec-only command `clock set 02:21:06 15 Oct 2026` before any
          measurement; during this case it read "Thu, 15 Oct 2026 02:33:57 +0000",
          Timezone UTC, offset +00:00.
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2 = swi_d (DUT); Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     T43853. No step text exists for this case in AWPTCM or ck.db, so the steps below
          were derived from, and graded against, the X960 software TPS row 25 "DHCP 120-day
          lease" (priority 1, supported = Yes); the wiki "DHCP" feature page, section "120
          Lease", verbatim: "Simple enhancement feature which allows a user to configure
          lease times up to 120 days. The maximum used to be 30 days (set by IP Infusion)."
          -- so the feature IS the raised ceiling, and the boundary is the heart of the
          case; the AW+ CLI page lease.md, `lease <days> <hours> <minutes> [<seconds>]`,
          "<days> The number of days, from 0 to 120 ... Default: 1", mode DHCP
          Configuration; and the DHCP Feature Overview and Configuration Guide
          C613-22102-00 REV G p13 "Set the lease". 5 steps plus a starting-state capture.
          120 days = 120 x 86400 = 10,368,000 s, the value DHCP option 51 must carry.
Build:    swi_d  sin_x96056-20261012-24  (flash:/x960-tb277.rel, build date
          Mon Oct 12 06:38:00 UTC 2026), AT-x960-56YSQ, S/N A2WQS700B, standalone Active
          Master, boot config flash:/default.cfg
Configs:  swi_d.cfg (running-config after setup, before step 1; saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- a 120-day lease is configurable and is delivered on the wire as DHCP
         option 51 = 10,368,000 s in both the OFFER and the ACK, the parser accepts 120 and
         refuses 121, and the setting survives a reboot.
  lease 120 0 0 accepted ............ yes          (no "% " line; pool reads <120:0:0:0>)
  option 51 in the OFFER ............ 10,368,000   (read out of the captured frame)
  option 51 in the ACK .............. 10,368,000   (read out of the captured frame)
  binding expiry .................... 12 Feb 2027 02:33:39 from 15 Oct 2026 02:33:39 UTC,
                                                    exactly 120 days to the second
  lease 121 0 0 ..................... refused      ("% Invalid input detected")
  lease 999 0 0 ..................... refused      ("% Invalid input detected")
  other lease values, same run ...... honoured     (1 day -> 86,400; 1 hour -> 3,600)
  no lease .......................... 1-day default restored on the wire (opt51 = 86,400)
                                                    and the lease line gone from the
                                                    running-config
  reboot ............................ <120:0:0:0> survives and still delivers 10,368,000
CAVEAT:  Options 58 (renewal / T1) and 59 (rebinding / T2) are ABSENT from every OFFER and
         ACK in this run, although the client explicitly requested them in its option 55
         parameter request list. They were recorded, not required: this server does not
         emit T1/T2 unless configured, so demanding them would have been a false FAIL.
         Nothing in this run measures what a client does with a 120-day lease over time --
         no lease was allowed to run to renewal, rebinding or expiry; the proof is that the
         configured duration is parsed, stored, delivered and re-delivered after a reboot.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

       tb277 eth3  00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
       scapy DHCP client (option 55 requests 1,3,6,51,58,59), tcpdump capture
              |    ^
     crafted  |    |  DHCPOFFER / DHCPACK -- option 51 is read out of
     DHCP     |    |  these two frames, and the verdict rests on them
     DISCOVER |    |
              v    |
    +-----------------------------------------------+
    |  swi_d   AT-x960-56YSQ                 DUT    |
    |  port1.0.10, untagged VLAN 10                 |
    |  interface vlan10  192.168.10.1/24            |
    |  service dhcp-server, pool CKPOOL             |
    |  range 192.168.10.100 - 192.168.10.110        |
    +-----------------------------------------------+

  DUT     : swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) console /dev/u2 @115200, S/N A2WQS700B, hostname awplus,
            standalone Active Master
  link    : tb277 eth3 <-> swi_d port1.0.10 (copper, untagged VLAN 10)
  host    : tb277 eth3, MAC 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e. Every frame is crafted and read at layer 2;
            no host IP, route, interface or mount was changed.
  unused  : swi_b (u3) and swi_a (u5) were never opened. The PDU (10.36.230.212 outlet 1)
            was not used; the reboot in STEP 5 was a CLI `reload`.
  tools   : tools/cfg.py (config driver, stops on any "% " line), tools/ckcon.py (show
            driver, used where a refusal had to be CAPTURED rather than abort the batch),
            tools/ckyn.py, tools/rc2cfg.py (produced swi_d.cfg), tools/rcdiff.py,
            tools/ckreload.py (answers `y` to reboot and `n` to any save-config question),
            tools/dhcp4.py (IPv4 DHCP client/server/decoder, "client" mode). All run on
            tb277 with python3.8.

  Method: a ping cannot read a lease time. Every step sends a crafted DHCPDISCOVER with a
  unique chaddr and an explicit option 55 parameter request list that ASKS for option 51
  (and 58/59), then reads option 51 out of the captured OFFER and the captured ACK.
  `show ip dhcp pool` and `show ip dhcp binding` are corroboration only, read after the
  wire and never instead of it.

